// The check that finds a name no function, no builtin and no variable owns.
//
// The check runs in the access pass rather than in the type checks, because
// the question "is this name a variable?" is answered by the declaration
// registry, and that is not built until check_names(). acc_callee already
// asks it for the one case idc.py singles out ("'x' is a variable, not a
// function"); this is the complement -- a name that is no function, no
// builtin and nobody's variable.

acc_callee2(int id, string owner, int ln) {
  if(k_of(id) == "call" && unresolved_fn(s1_of(id)) == 1) {
    report_unknown_fn(s1_of(id), owner, ln);
  }
} return void;

// Deliberately not is_unknown_fn: that one names the builtins it knows about
// and is allowed to fall behind, because falling behind only makes *its*
// check quieter. Here a missed builtin would be reported as a typo, so the
// question is asked of the real list instead.
unresolved_fn(string name) {
  int ok = 0;
  if(find_str((import fnames), name) < 0 && is_builtin(name) == 0 && find_str((import dvname), name) < 0) {
    ok = 1;
  }
} return int ok;
